Impact Analysis: Removing Azure APIM from Current Architecture
Current Workflow
User → Cloudflare → Firewall → Azure APIM → AKS Internal LB (Ingress) → Istio Ingress → Pod

What Happens If We Remove Azure APIM?
New Workflow
User → Cloudflare → Firewall → AKS Internal LB (Ingress) → Istio Ingress → Pod

Context

APIs are publicly exposed

All traffic already enters through Cloudflare

AKS uses Istio for internal traffic control

APIM currently performs path prefix abstraction

Path Handling (Important Migration Consideration)
Current Behavior (With APIM + Istio)

External request:

marketdata.xyz.com/md/health

Current Flow

APIM exposes /md/*

APIM forwards traffic to AKS

Istio VirtualService has:

match: prefix /md/

rewrite: /

Backend service ultimately receives:

/health


So today:

/md/* is externally visible

Istio performs the rewrite rule (prefix /md/ → rewrite /)

APIM effectively acts as the public API entry abstraction, while Istio performs the actual path rewrite.

Migration Plan (Without APIM)

Before removing APIM:

Ensure Istio VirtualService continues to support:

match: prefix /md/

rewrite: /

Confirm Cloudflare forwards the full /md/* path unchanged

Validate backend services still receive:

/health

This Guarantees

No endpoint changes for external consumers

No breaking API contracts

Seamless transition

